\section{Mismatched Decoding for Finite Alphabets}\label{Mismatched Decoding for Finite Alphabets}
\subsection{Finite-Alphabet Gaussian Channel under Interference}
We consider coded communications with finite-alphabet inputs under additive interference and additive Gaussian noise, or FAGCI. Without loss of generality, we assume that there are two interference sources. Thus, at time instance $t$, the received signal can be expressed as
\begin{equation}\label{FAG channel}
    y_t = x_t + i_t + j_t + z_t,
\end{equation}
where $x_t$ and $y_t$ are the complex-valued channel input and output, $i_t$ and $j_t$ are the two complex-valued interference observations, and $z_t$ is the noise observation. $x_t$, $i_t$ and $j_t$ are uniformly drawn from alphabets $\mathcal{X}$, $\mathcal{I}$ and $\mathcal{J}$ correspondingly\footnote{This work focus on uniformly distributed inputs and interference for clarity. Extension to non-uniformly distributions is straightforward.}, while $z_t$ are samples of $Z_t\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\;\sigma_z^2)$. Unless any of $\mathcal{X}$, $\mathcal{I}$ and $\mathcal{J}$ is $\{0\}$, $\mathbb{E}\left[|X_t|^2\right] = \sigma_x^2$, $\mathbb{E}\left[|I_t|^2\right] = \sigma_i^2$ and $\mathbb{E}\left[|J_t|^2\right] = \sigma_j^2$. We focus on memoryless channels without feedback, hence
\begin{equation}
    f_{\mathbf{Y}|\mathbf{X}}(\mathbf{y}|\mathbf{x}) = \prod_{t=1}^n f_{Y|X}(y_t|x_t),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ are the input and output sequences of length $n$. Henceforth, the time index is omitted if no ambiguity arises. 

