\subsection{Benchmarking architectures}
\label{sec:benchmarkingarchitectures}
\noindent
Table \ref{tab:slidingtrue} presents a comparison of different network architectures trained with \code{Sliding=True}. We observe that the ChannelCat architecture obtains excellent performance, in SR almost approaching the levels of DEBiT-b, which, however, benefits from a more complex architecture based on cross-attention, and in particular pre-training on cross-view completion and RPVE. Without pre-training, the DEBiT architecture is not exploitable, as also reported in \cite{CrocoNav2024}. Both ResNet9 and ViT Late Fusion architectures underperform, which we explain by the simplicity of comparing observation and goal image late, at the outputs of the encoder $\phi$. Adding SpaceToDepth does not seem to have a significant impact neither on ChannelCat nor on Late Fusion ResNet9: being able to compare local patches across images in a single layer through convolutions does not translate into numerical gains.


In Table \ref{tab:slidingfalse} we present the same comparisons when agents are trained with \code{Sliding=False}, and the trends change dramatically. While the impact on the (previously already underperforming) Late Fusion architecture is similar, ChannelCat  now breaks down and performance is halved, or less. ChannelCat does not seem to help, nor its combination with SpaceToDepth for ResNet9. DEBiT, on the other hand, is able to cope well with the more realistic simulator setting: while performance drops slightly, SR $90.5{\rightarrow}81.7$, it is still high, and in line with what is reported in the original paper \cite{CrocoNav2024} (see $\mathsection$ in the caption of Table \ref{tab:slidingfalse}).


\subsection{Transferring capabilities obtained w. ``Sliding''}
\noindent
The high impact of the \code{Sliding} simulator setting came as a big surprise to us. While its role in sim2real transfer of point goal navigation was established in \cite{kadian20sm2real}, the common conjecture was that it impacted ``raw'' navigation performance, \ie, finding free space, avoiding obstacles, short, capabilities which are independent of the extraction of goal directional information. Our experiments, however, suggest that the particular choice of binocular visual encoder $\phi$ is impacted by this hyper-parameter, which in theory should be related to motion only.


