\stitle{Metrics.}
The overall evaluation metrics involve both search efficiency and accuracy. For the search efficiency metric, we use query per second (Qps), which indicates the number of queries processed by the algorithm per second to evaluate all the methods as it is most commonly used in the benchmark. For the search accuracy, we use \textit{recall} defined in~\S~\ref{subsec:defn} as the metric to align with the baselines~\cite{Acorn-SIGMOD-2024,UNG-SIGMOD-2025}.
All metrics used in the experiment are reported on averages.


\stitle{Label Distribution.}
In real scenarios, keywords, tags, and labels often approximate the power law distribution. Previous work adopts the power law distribution, the Zipf distribution, to generate labels~\cite{Filtered-diskann-WWW-2023,UNG-SIGMOD-2025}. We use its original code to generate label data for base vectors with varying possible labels of $|\mathcal{L}|=$8, 12, 24, and 32. We also consider real datasets and other label distributions, such as Uniform and Poisson, in subsequent experiments.


\stitle{Algorithms.}
The algorithms compared in our study are as follows:

\noindent
$\bullet$
$\ELI$-0.2: Our proposed method with fixed search efficiency: elastic factor bound set to 0.2.

\noindent
$\bullet$
$\ELI$-2.0: Our proposed method with fixed index space: use at most double the original index space.

\noindent
$\bullet$
$\UNG$: Unified navigating graph approach based on label navigation graph~\cite{UNG-SIGMOD-2025}.

\noindent
$\bullet$
$\ACORN$-1: ANN constraint-optimized retrieval network with low construction overhead~\cite{Acorn-SIGMOD-2024}.

\noindent
$\bullet$
$\ACORN$-$\gamma$: ANN constraint-optimized retrieval network for high-efficiency search~\cite{Acorn-SIGMOD-2024}.

\stitle{Implementation Details.}
All code was implemented in C++ and compiled using GCC version 9.4.0 with -Ofast optimization. 
The experiments were conducted on a workstation with Intel(R) Xeon(R) Platinum 8352V CPUs @ 2.10GHz, 512GB of memory.
We utilized multi-threading (144 threads) for index construction and a single thread for search evaluation. We use $\HNSW$ as the modular index with parameters M=16 and \textit{efconstruct}=200. For $\ELI$-0.2(<1.0), we use the index selection method to achieve a fixed elastic factor of 0.2. For $\ELI$-2.0(>1.0), we use the fixed space method of at most double the origin index space to achieve the maximum efficiency. For other baselines such as $\ACORN$ and $\UNG$, we use the default parameters in their papers, i.e., $\alpha$=1.2 L=100 for $\UNG$ and $\gamma=12$ for $\ACORN$-$\gamma$.


