\section{Related Work}

\stitle{Vector Similarity Search.}
Vector similarity search has been widely studied. Most of the research focuses on approximate search~\cite{LSH-1999}, as exact search has a high cost in high-dimensional space~\cite{Curse-of-dim-1998}. Among them, $c$-approximate nearest neighbor search returns the result with an approximate ratio of $c$, which can be solved by local sensitive hashing (LSH) in sublinear time~\cite{LSH-Pstable-2004,RPLSH-1995,LSH-1999}. For the AKNN search problem in this paper, graph-based vector indexes~\cite{NSG-VLDB-2019-deng-cai,SSG-PAMI-2022-deng-cai,Starling-SIGMOD-2024-Mengzhao,tMRNG:journals/pacmmod/PengCCYX23,Diskann-NIPS-2019,HNSW-PAMI-2020,HVS-VLDB-2021-kejing-lu,SymphonyQG-SIGMOD-2025} are the current state-of-the-art solution, which is much more efficient than LSH according to benchmark~\cite{ANNSurvey-TKDE-2020-Wei-Wang}. In addition, inverted list-based indexes~\cite{PQ-PAMI-2014,IMI-PAMI-2015} and quantization-based methods~\cite{PQ-PAMI-2014,OPQ-PAMI-2014,LOPQ-2014-CVPR,ITQ:/pami/GongLGP13,Rabitq-SIGMOD-2024,ExRaBitQ-arxiv-2024,MRQ-RESQ-arxiv-2024-Mingyu} are also widely used in different scenarios of AKNN search. For example, inverted indexes use less space, and quantization methods can speed up the distance computation of AKNN search~\cite{BSA-DDC-ICDE-2024-Mingyu,Graph-Index-Accelearte-SIGMOD-2025-Mengzhao}. We use the most widely used $\HNSW$ algorithm~\cite{hnswlib} as the module index in this paper, and other well-optimized AKNN search libraries~\cite{Diskann-NIPS-2019,VSAG-arxiv-2025} can also replace it.

\stitle{Attribute-filtering AKNN search.}
Various ways of AKNN indexing support attribute value filtering, where the attribute can be a label, a keyword, or a numerical value. We divided them into two classes: the attribute-free and attribute-involved approach.

\sstitle{Attribute-Free Approach.} These methods do not require attributes during index construction and determine whether a vector is filtered on the fly. Based on this, the $\PRE$ and $\POST$ search strategies have been proposed. These two strategies have different performances under different queries. Database systems such as ADB~\cite{AnalyticDB-VLDB-2020}, VBASE~\cite{Vbase-OSDI-2023}, CHASE~\cite{CHASE-arxiv-Sean-Wang}, Milvus~\cite{Milvus-SIGMOD-2021}
etc., select different strategies based on the cost model. The $\ACORN$ is also based on the $\PRE$ strategy, but builds a denser graph index to avoid the connectivity issue caused by the $\PRE$ strategy.
Since the above strategies completely ignore the attributes during index construction, the search accuracy and efficiency have a large gap compared to the method that involves attributes.

\sstitle{Attribute-Involved Approach.} The attribute-involved index is constructed based on different attribute types. For range filter queries on numerical attributes, recent methods~\cite{iRangeGraph-SIGMOD-2025,Window-Filter-ICML-2024,ESG-arxiv-2025-Mingyu} construct a segment tree to reconstruct the query interval. SeRF~\cite{SeRF-SIGMOD-2024} uses the idea of compression to compress the index corresponding to each query range interval into a compact one. For label attributes, $\UNG$~\cite{UNG-SIGMOD-2025} uses the inclusion relationship of the label set and constructs cross-group edges to ensure that the graph index only searches the filtered vectors. 
Beyond the above methods, NHQ~\cite{NHQ-NIPS-2022-mengzhao-wang} takes the attribute as part of the vector similarity computation, which requires manually adjusting the weight of the two parts. The recent approach DEG~\cite{DEG-SIGMOD-2025} solves this by adjusting the graph neighbor dynamically.

\stitle{The Index Selection Problem.}
Materialized view/index selection is a critical problem in many applications that has been studied for decades~\cite{Impl-data-cube-SIGMOD-1996,View-Select-SIGMOD-2012}. Various greedy algorithms have been proposed to solve this problem, such as~\cite{Impl-data-cube-SIGMOD-1996}, maximizing the benefit of selecting views at each round. Different from previous studies, we study the efficiency-oriented index selection scheme, use elastic factors to model efficiency, and provide a space-based cost function, making full use of the existing vector index theory and features. Finally, we provide index selection under space constraints. In the future, we will consider index selection under changes in query workload and more advanced index selection algorithms.

\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we study the label-hybrid approximate nearest neighbor search problem, especially the query for the label-containing scenario. Our first contribution is to propose an index sharing scheme. This scheme is based on the finding that the existing vector index has guaranteed performance in theory and practice when the index elements have a certain overlap rate. Then we define the fixed efficiency index selection problem, taking the index space as the cost function. 
Our second contribution is to propose a greedy index selection algorithm to optimize the index space under the requirement of fixed efficiency. Moreover, we extend our approach to maximize efficiency in the scenario of fixed space. Our algorithm is highly versatile and can be combined with existing well-optimized libraries while having higher search efficiency.
