The key observation is that for a Poisson process the expected distance to the next even is independent of the distance since the last event. The typical example is to consider the time betwen phone calls arriving at a switchboard -- the expected time until the next call is independent of the time since the prior call (the process is ``memoryless''). In soaring we would interpret this to mean that the expected distance to the next thermal is independent of the distance traveled since the prior thermal. The effect of this is that when considering inter-event spacing it does not matter what point is chosen for consideration. We can thus let disk $\mathcal{D}$ be centered on any point (not only on thermals) without affecting its area. Thus the relationship in equation \ref{eqn:percolation_disk} holds for any point. The distance $r_1$ thus represents the expected distance between any point in the plane and a thermal, and the intensity of a point process describing the occurrence of thermals along a flight path can be given $\lambda_1 = \frac{1}{r_1}$. This then permits the 1 and 2D equivalence to be completed:
