\begin{abstract}

Artificial Intelligence (AI) Infrastructures, represented by Deep Learning (DL) frameworks, have served as fundamental DL systems over the last decade. However, the bugs in DL frameworks could lead to catastrophic consequences in some critical scenarios (\eg healthcare and autonomous driving). A simple yet effective way to find bugs in DL frameworks is fuzz testing (Fuzzing). Unfortunately, existing fuzzing techniques have not comprehensively considered multiple types of feedback (\eg coverage information and exception logs). Additionally, they analyze feedback in a coarse-grained manner, such as mutating the test cases only according to whether the coverage increases. Recently, researchers introduced Large Language Models (LLMs) into fuzzing, showing their promising performance in generating test cases for systems under test (SUTs). However, current LLM-based fuzzing techniques only focus on using LLMs to generate test cases while overlooking their potential to analyze feedback information, failing to create more valid and diverse test cases.

To fill this gap, we propose \tactic to break the seal of \underline{\textbf{F}}eedback-driven f\underline{\textbf{U}}zzing for d\underline{\textbf{E}}ep \underline{\textbf{L}}earning frameworks. The backbone of \tactic comprises two LLM-based agents, namely analysis LLM and generation LLM. Analysis LLM agent infers analysis summaries from feedback information (\ie coverage information and exception logs) while the generation LLM agent creates tests guided by these analysis summaries. Subsequently, based on the multiple types of feedback, we design two mechanisms to improve model diversity and validity: \textit{(i)} a heuristic search algorithm to enhance test generation, enriching model diversity; \textit{(ii)} a program self-repair strategy to automatically repair invalid models with exception logs. We evaluate \tactic on two widely-used DL frameworks (\ie \pt and \tf) and experiment results show that \tactic can improve line code coverage of \pt and \tf by 9.15\% and 14.70\% over state-of-the-art baselines. \revise{So far, \tactic has detected 104 bugs for \pt and \tf, with 93 confirmed as new bugs, 47 already fixed, and 5 assigned with CVE IDs.} Our work indicates that considering multiple types of feedback is beneficial to fuzzing performance and leveraging LLMs to analyze feedback information is a promising direction.

\end{abstract}

