\vspace{3pt}
\noindent
\textbf{Training Objective.}
We formulate the recommendation as a conditional language modeling task. Given a user's interaction history $\mathcal{H}_i$ and dialog context $c^{(i,k)}_{t}$, the model predicts the \(j\)-th token $s_{t}^{(j)}$ at turn \(t\) from the vocabulary. 
We generate a full turn \(t\) by recursing until a special token \texttt{<EOS>} is generated.
\[
\mathcal{L} = -\sum_{j=1} \log P(s_{t}^{(j)} \mid s_{t}^{(<j)}, c_t^{(i,k)} \mathcal{H}_i; \theta),
\]
where $\theta$ are the model parameters. The final recommendation is generated in the free-form text generation style, where the system act is \texttt{Recommend}.
When we have access to a set of candidate items, we can directly compute the log-probability over item tokens.



\noindent
\textbf{Training Details.}
We initialize the model from a GPT-2 small variant (117M) and fine-tune it on our generated datasets. Item titles are pre-tokenized using Byte Pair Encoding (BPE). 
We use a batch size of 32, learning rate of 5e-5 with linear warm-up, and train for 5 epochs. Generation during evaluation uses nucleus sampling with $p=0.9$ and top-$k=50$. 
The cross-attention is applied at every decoder layer to enable multi-level alignment between history and dialog context.


